gyS(){return"Does LastStats scrobble my music?"},
gyE(){return"No. LastStats is a visualisation app: it displays the scrobbles already recorded on your Last.fm account, but it does not record any itself.\n\nTo automatically scrobble your music, use a dedicated app such as Pano Scrobbler (available on Android)."},
gyY(){return"Does the app work on macOS or other platforms?"},
gyK(){return"LastStats is developed and tested on Android. Behaviour on other platforms (macOS, Windows, Linux\u2026) is unverified, bugs or unexpected behaviour may occur."},
gyZ(){return"Is LastStats open source?"},
gyL(){return"Yes! The source code is freely available on GitHub. The project is independent, built with passion by SanoBld. Feel free to contribute, report bugs, or leave a star \u2b50."},
gz_(){return"Where is my data stored?"},
gyM(){return"On your device only. LastStats has no server: your scrobbles are cached locally for fast access, and your Last.fm credentials are stored locally too. Nothing is sent anywhere except Last.fm's official API."},
gz0(){return"How do I enable favorites?"},
gyN(){return"Go to Settings > Account and enter your Last.fm secret key (you can find it next to your API key at last.fm/api/accounts), then follow the steps on screen. Once connected, you can favorite tracks directly from the app. This is not available with the app's built-in key."},
gz1(){return"What is a 'scrobble'?"},
gyO(){return"A scrobble is a track logged as played on your Last.fm account \u2014 it's Last.fm's own term for 'one counted listen'. All your totals (top artists, stats, etc.) are based on it."},
gz2(){return"How do levels and achievements work?"},
gyP(){return"Your account level grows with your total scrobble count (there's no max level). Cards also get a border (bronze \u2192 iridescent) based on how many times that artist/track/album has been played. Everything is computed automatically from stats already cached locally, with no extra network calls."},
gz3(){return"How does power saving mode work?"},
gyQ(){return"Power saving mode spaces out automatic syncs to save battery. It can be always on, follow your phone's own power saving mode, or turn on below a battery level you choose, from Settings > General."},
gyT(){return"How do I back up or restore my data?"},
gyF(){return"Go to Settings > Backup. You can export a backup file, with or without your Last.fm key as you prefer, and import it later on this phone or on another device to get your settings back."},
gyU(){return"Does the app work offline?"},
gyG(){return"Yes, to some extent. Stats already loaded stay available offline thanks to local caching, but a connection is still needed to fetch new scrobbles."},
gyV(){return"Can I switch Last.fm accounts?"},
gyH(){return'Yes, you can save up to 3 Last.fm accounts. From Settings > Account, tap "Add an account" and then switch between them whenever you like. The local cache is automatically reset when you switch, so the data of two accounts never gets mixed.'},
gyW(){return"How do I set up notifications?"},
gyI(){return"From Settings > Notifications, you can turn on an alert when a sync finishes, choose how often alerts appear, or turn notifications off completely if you prefer."},
gyX(){return"Artwork is missing or keeps loading. What can I do?"},
gyJ(){return"Clear the cache in the app (Settings > Cache), then in Android (Settings > Apps > LastStats > Storage > Clear cache). If images are still missing, back up your data (Settings > Backup), uninstall and reinstall the app, then restore your backup."},
